{# ===========================================================================
   Component: hdr

   THE site header. Included by layouts/base.njk, so every page renders this
   exact file: there is no second header variant that can drift from it.

   Two changes against the earlier four-item header, signed off 2026-08-03:
     1. Locations becomes a disclosure that expands to the seven rooms.
     2. Order Online joins the top level as the header's single red CTA.

   Everything in the panel comes from locations.json. North Park is absent
   because it is not in locations.json, which is the correct reason: it is
   closed, and nothing should have to remember to exclude it.

   ACCESSIBILITY
   The trigger is a real <button> with aria-expanded and aria-controls, not a
   link with a hover handler, so it works from the keyboard and announces its
   state. Escape closes and returns focus to the trigger. The panel is
   `hidden` when closed, so its links are out of the tab order entirely rather
   than merely invisible.

   Each card holds two separate links, a card link and an order link, as
   siblings. They are never nested, which would be invalid and would make the
   inner one unreachable.
   =========================================================================== #}

{#
  `megaInstance` disambiguates the ids when the header is rendered more than
  once in one document, which /nav-preview/ does (interactive at the top, and
  again forced open below). Without it both copies claim
  id="mega-locations-trigger", the ids are no longer unique, and aria-controls
  and aria-labelledby resolve to whichever the parser saw first. Real pages
  render the header once and never set this.
#}
{%- set megaId = "mega-" + (megaInstance | default("")) %}
<header class="hdr hdr--mega">
  <div class="hdr__in">
    {#
      Two assets, one per theme, swapped by CSS in hdr.css. NEVER a filter: a
      filter-based fake was shipped in an earlier draft and turned the mark
      pink. Both files are horizontal lockups at 2.25:1 and 2.34:1, so the swap
      costs about 4% of width and no layout shift.

      alt lives on one image only. Both are in the DOM at all times and CSS
      hides the inactive one, so two alt strings would announce the brand name
      twice. The hidden one is decorative by definition.
    #}
    <a class="hdr__logo" href="/" aria-label="{{ site.name }} home">
      <img
        class="hdr__logo-img hdr__logo-dark"
        src="/images/brand/tajima-logo-header-dark.png"
        alt="{{ site.logo.alt }}"
        width="800"
        height="356"
        fetchpriority="high">
      <img
        class="hdr__logo-img hdr__logo-light"
        src="/images/brand/tajima-logo-header-light.png"
        alt=""
        width="800"
        height="342"
        fetchpriority="high">

      {#
        THE NIGHT SIGN. Two words under the wordmark, brush italic, glowing
        red, pulsing slowly like a tube warming up. Night only: CSS sets it
        display:none in day mode, which takes it out of the accessibility tree
        as well as out of the picture, so in day it is not there in any sense.

        It is decoration and says nothing, so aria-hidden even at night. It is
        NOT a statement about hours: the words are only "At Night" and no time
        goes near it. The rooms' actual hours live on the location pages and in
        openingHoursSpecification.

        Inside the logo link and absolutely positioned, so it costs the header
        no height and the nav does not move when the clock flips the theme.
        See .hdr__neon in hdr.css for the size it had to be and why.
      #}
      <span class="hdr__neon" aria-hidden="true">At Night</span>
    </a>

    <nav class="hdr__nav" aria-label="Primary">
      {%- for item in nav.primary | nonEmpty("nav.primary") %}
        {%- set isCurrent = page.url == item.url %}

        {%- if item.mega %}
        {# ---------------- Locations, the disclosure ---------------- #}
        <div class="mega" data-mega>
          <button
            type="button"
            class="hdr__nav-link mega__trigger{% if isCurrent %} hdr__nav-link--current{% endif %}"
            id="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-trigger"
            aria-expanded="false"
            aria-controls="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-panel"
            data-mega-trigger>
            {{ item.label }}
            <svg class="mega__chevron" width="10" height="7" viewBox="0 0 10 7" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M1 1.5 5 5.5 9 1.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </button>

          <div
            class="mega__panel"
            id="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-panel"
            role="group"
            aria-labelledby="{{ megaId }}{{ item.mega }}-trigger"
            data-mega-panel
            hidden>
            <div class="mega__inner">

              <p class="mega__lede">
                Six rooms across San Diego, one in Kihei. Walk in, no reservations.
              </p>

              <ul class="mega__grid">
                {%- for loc in locations.items %}
                <li class="mega__cell">
                  <article class="loc-card">
                    <a class="loc-card__link" href="{{ loc.url }}">
                      {#
                        PLACEHOLDER, and openly one. The same real Tajima
                        photograph in all seven cards, from roomPhotos._placeholder.

                        Replaced the drawn hatched panel on 2026-08-05. It was
                        the right call while nothing had been placed, but only
                        two of the seven rooms have photography of themselves,
                        so per-location photos would have shown two real rooms
                        and five holding slots. An inconsistent mix reads as
                        broken; one generic dish repeated reads as a known
                        placeholder state. Consistency across the seven is
                        worth more than any single slot being "correct" right
                        now.

                        Karaage because menu.json has it at all seven and
                        CLIENT_FACTS.md calls it the most universally
                        available izakaya item across all locations. It is a
                        real Tajima photograph, not stock and not AI, so it
                        does not touch the DESIGN_SYSTEM.md ban. It is not
                        location-tagged in the manifest, which is what keeps
                        it clear of the rule that a location-named photo only
                        goes on that location's page: no Convoy frame could
                        sit in the College Heights card.

                        alt="" and aria-hidden because HERE it is decorative.
                        It says nothing about which room this is, and seven
                        identical alt strings would be noise in a links list.
                        The card's accessible name comes from the location
                        name below. Real alt text lives in photos.json.

                        WHEN THE ROOMS ARE SHOT: give each card its own photo
                        and its own real alt, and delete this block. Do not
                        keep it for the stragglers.
                      #}
                      {#- PREVIEW BRANCH: each card shows its own exterior where
                          one exists, falling back to the shared dish for the
                          rooms with none (Maui). Still aria-hidden and alt="":
                          the card's accessible name is the location name below,
                          and seven alt strings in a links list would be noise.

                          loading="lazy" is load-bearing here. This panel ships
                          `hidden` and is revealed by JS, so nothing is fetched
                          until someone opens the menu: measured at roughly
                          60 KB deferred across seven cards, 0 bytes on first
                          paint. Do not change it to eager. -#}
                      {%- set cardPhoto = roomPhotos[loc.id].hero or roomPhotos._placeholder %}
                      <span class="loc-card__media" aria-hidden="true">
                        <img
                          class="loc-card__media-img"
                          src="{{ cardPhoto.src }}"
                          alt=""
                          loading="lazy"
                          decoding="async"
                          width="{{ cardPhoto.width }}"
                          height="{{ cardPhoto.height }}">
                        <span class="loc-card__media-no">{{ "0" if loop.index < 10 }}{{ loop.index }}</span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="loc-card__name">{{ loc.name }}</span>
                    </a>

                    {#
                      WHAT MAKES THIS ROOM DIFFERENT FROM THE OTHER SIX, and
                      the live answer to "can I go now".

                      Added 2026-09-14. Every card carried a neighborhood,
                      Directions and Order Online, which is the same three
                      things seven times: nothing on the card helped anyone
                      choose between rooms, which is the only decision this
                      panel exists to support.

                      `descriptor` is locations.json's own one-line field, two
                      or three words each, already written and already
                      distinguishing ("The original", "Izakaya and bar", "Walk
                      to Petco"). Nothing is authored here.

                      SUPPRESSED WHERE IT WOULD REPEAT THE LINE BELOW. Maui's
                      descriptor is "Kihei" and its place line is also "Kihei",
                      so that card would read the word twice. Same collision
                      rule the place line itself already follows for Crown
                      Point. The data is not wrong and is not changed; only
                      what gets printed is.
                    #}
                    {%- set placeLine = loc.neighborhood if (loc.neighborhood and loc.neighborhood != loc.name) else loc.address.locality %}
                    {%- if loc.descriptor and loc.descriptor != placeLine %}
                    <p class="loc-card__desc">{{ loc.descriptor }}</p>
                    {%- endif %}

                    {#
                      THE LIVE STATUS, the same mechanism as the location pages:
                      ships hidden and empty, and /src/js/hours.js fills it from
                      the same `hours` array that feeds openingHoursSpecification,
                      computed in America/Los_Angeles. It is client-side only and
                      never enters schema.

                      It is the most useful thing on the card to someone choosing
                      between rooms at 9pm, and it costs nothing: the code was
                      already shipping for the location pages.

                      Maui has no hours, so it renders no element at all rather
                      than a fallback string, exactly as it does in the NAP plate.
                    #}
                    {%- if loc.hours and loc.hours.length %}
                    <span class="loc-card__status" data-hours-status data-hours='{{ loc.hours | dump }}' hidden></span>
                    {%- elif loc.closesAt %}
                    {#
                      MAUI. A CLOSING TIME, NOT A LIVE STATUS, and the two do
                      not look alike on purpose.

                      That room has no `hours` array: Connor's 2026-09-09
                      confirmation covered six of seven and Maui was not
                      supplied, so it emits no openingHoursSpecification and
                      js/hours.js cannot compute whether it is open right now.
                      What we do have, from Steve on 2026-09-14, is the hour it
                      shuts. The card says exactly that and no more.

                      No dot, because the dot on the other six means "computed
                      from today's window" and this is not that. See
                      _closesAtSource in locations.json for what would have to
                      arrive before this row becomes a real status.
                    #}
                    <span class="loc-card__status loc-card__status--static">Closes {{ loc.closesAt | clockTime }}</span>
                    {%- endif %}

                    {#
                      Routes STRAIGHT TO TOAST as of 2026-08-05, no longer via
                      /order-online/#<slug>. The click-test that unblocked this
                      confirmed all seven destinations and settled the two
                      competing URL patterns on one: order.toasttab.com/online/
                      <slug>. See locations.json _orderUrlStatus.

                      Still one place to change a URL: the href is
                      loc.orderUrl, and /order-online/ reads the same field, so
                      the two surfaces cannot drift. /order-online/ stays a real
                      page (5,648 clicks); it is simply no longer a waypoint.

                      Two slugs are knowingly wrong and knowingly kept, Crown
                      Point's reading tajima-pacific-beach and Plaza Bonita's
                      reading suite-2075. Both are Toast's internal routing, not
                      NAP. Do not tidy them here; see orderUrlNote.

                      The visually-hidden span gives each of the seven buttons a
                      distinct accessible name, since "Order online" seven times
                      is useless in a links list.
                    #}
                    {#
                      Neighborhood and Directions share one line: the
                      neighborhood reads from the left edge and Directions is
                      pinned to the right, so the link sits in the same place
                      on all seven cards. It used to be "hood, middot,
                      Directions" in reading order, which put the link at a
                      different x in every card. The middot is gone with the
                      arrangement that needed it; see .loc-card__hood.

                      The neighborhood had to come OUT of .loc-card__link to do
                      this: the card is already a link and an anchor cannot
                      nest inside an anchor.

                      Muted and not underlined at rest, per the brief. The
                      external-link glyph is therefore the only at-rest cue
                      that this is a link, so an underline comes back on hover
                      and focus and the focus ring is untouched.
                    #}
                    {#
                      Crown Point's neighborhood IS "Crown Point", so printing
                      both would render "CROWN POINT / Crown Point · Directions".
                      Same duplication that produced "National City, National
                      City" on /order-online/. Where they collide, the locality
                      carries the line instead. The data is not wrong and is not
                      changed; only what gets printed is.
                    #}
                    <p class="loc-card__hood">
                      <span class="loc-card__place">{{ placeLine }}</span>
                      <a class="link-quiet" href="{{ loc | mapsUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                        Directions<span class="u-visually-hidden"> to {{ loc.name }}, opens in a new tab</span>
                        <svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                          <path d="M5 1.5h7.5V9M12.5 1.5 5.5 8.5M9 12.5H1.5V5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                        </svg>
                      </a>
                    </p>

                    {# The one button in the card. Straight to that kitchen's
                       Toast page. All seven have a URL, so there is no
                       fallback branch to write. #}
                    <a class="loc-card__order" href="{{ loc.orderUrl }}" rel="noopener">
                      Order online<span class="u-visually-hidden"> from {{ loc.name }}</span>
                    </a>
                  </article>
                </li>
                {%- endfor %}
              </ul>

              {#
                TIJUANA IS A LINE AND AN OUTBOUND LINK, NEVER AN EIGHTH CARD.
                Added 2026-09-14 on Steve's instruction, in the same treatment
                /locations/ already uses for it, and for the same reasons:
                SITE_ARCHITECTURE.md says "Tijuana: outbound link to
                tajimamx.com. Not a card", CLIENT_FACTS.md has the Tijuana
                operating count unresolved (older materials reference up to
                four), and voice-tone.md forbids publishing a global total. A
                card in this grid would number it and assert a count.

                tajimamx.com is a separate property on a separate domain, in
                Spanish, so this leaves the site: rel="external noopener" and
                an out-of-tab cue, the same as the Directions links above.
              #}
              <p class="mega__foot">
                <a class="mega__foot-link" href="{{ item.url }}">All locations, with addresses and phone numbers</a>
                <span class="mega__foot-aside">
                  South of the border?
                  <a class="mega__foot-link mega__foot-link--out" href="https://tajimamx.com" rel="external noopener" target="_blank">
                    Visit Tajima Tijuana<span class="u-visually-hidden">, opens tajimamx.com in a new tab</span>
                    <svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
                      <path d="M5 1.5h7.5V9M12.5 1.5 5.5 8.5M9 12.5H1.5V5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    </svg>
                  </a>
                </span>
              </p>

            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        {%- elif item.cta %}
        {# ---------------- Order Online, the header CTA ---------------- #}
        <a
          class="hdr__nav-link hdr__nav-link--cta"
          href="{{ item.url }}"
          {%- if isCurrent %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
          {{ item.label }}
        </a>

        {%- else %}
        <a
          class="hdr__nav-link{% if item.sub %} hdr__nav-link--sub{% endif %}{% if isCurrent %} hdr__nav-link--current{% endif %}"
          href="{{ item.url }}"
          {%- if isCurrent %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
          {{- item.label }}{% if item.sub %} <span aria-hidden="true">&#8629;</span>{% endif -%}
        </a>
        {%- endif %}

      {%- endfor %}
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
